Well, that could narrow the field down - find a book where the
white wizards /aren't/ total badasses.

The Tomorrow People, who had a kids' TV show and
novelisations by, um, Roger Price?, had a similar limitation.  
They're the next stage in human development, and their powers
include telepathy, telekinesis, and teleportation.

Wizards... Lord Darcy's magic-users in Randall Garrett's stories,
which I just re-read, consider harmful unethical magic to be Black
Magic, inevitably corrupting and damaging to the soul and
regarded by the educated majority as an absolute "not do".  
They are quite serious about Christian morality, and consider it
measurable, although we're also shown Jewish and, I forget,
maybe Muslim magical professionals.  IIRC, you only get to
become a priest in Lord Darcy's world if you have some of the
magic Talent, and all Healers (who practise laying on of hands)
are priests.

Actual Black Magic is rarely seen in the stories, although some
murders or suspicious deaths depend on magic which makes
the victim vulnerable - but when it is practised, the practitioner
doesn't immediately explode or fall down dead.  (Which I guess is
goiung to make it even more rarely practised; only by suicide
bomber wizards, eh?)  So it's not these.

Kind of like how when a bee stings you, the bee dies... maybe not
everyone knew that.  (And I'm not sure it's true of all the different
kinds of bee.)

There are hundreds, maybe thousands, of Extruded Fantasy
Product concerning wizards which I haven't read, nor do I intend
to, so I don't doubt the existence of the Aieee!-For-An-Eye magical
world described in the original posting.

Incidentally, another paradox that I felt was how in the _Star Wars_
universe, it's seen as natural that robots, and humanoid robots
with the power of speech in particular, are often used as soldiers,
assassins, etc.  I think it must be that I was so strongly impressed
by Isaac Asimov's Three Laws - which other writers happily
borrowed with or without acknowledgement - that a robot capable
of cheerfully killing human beings is to me not just creepy, but a
"does not compute" concept - even though Asimov himself put an
intentionally homicidal robot in one of his stories.  And there are
killer robots in other fictions, but most of them don't talk, or aren't
humanoid, or are unique.  In Star Wars, there are mass-produced
standard models of killer robot.

